    "passage": "Generally covered in bright pink feathers, flamingos are long-legged wading birds. With a name [[1]] from a Spanish or Portuguese word meaning “flame-colored,” flamingos are known for their vibrant appearance. Despite being the bird’s best-known [[2]], the pink feathers are actually not inherited. Instead, gray is the natural color of flamingos when they are born. So, if it’s not part of their DNA, why do these birds [[3]] shades of pink?\nThe phrase “You are what you eat” rings [[4]] to flamingos than to humans. Beta-carotene, a red orange pigment found in large amounts within the algae, brine fly larvae and brine shrimp that flamingos eat in their wetland [[5]], gives the bird the bright pink color. In the digestive system, enzymes [[6]] carotenoids into pigments which are absorbed by fats in the liver and deposited in the feathers and skin of flamingos. To actually color those [[7]] appearances, carotenoids must be ingested in very large amounts. Having a diet almost entirely [[8]] of carotenoid-packed delicacies, flamingos have no problem coloring themselves. For humans to give their skin an orange hue, on the other hand, they would have to consume quite a lot of carrots — a food so [[9]] in carotenoids that it gave the pigments their name.\nThere are six different [[10]] of flamingos, which can be found in different parts of the world. As environments and food sources differ from place to place and season to season, the pink color of flamingos also varies greatly. Interestingly, a flamingo’s color matters when mating season comes around — the pinker the bird, the easier it can find a mate since that means it is healthy and good at foraging.",
